    "question": "Which of the following definitions correctly describes an Abstract Data Type (ADT)?",
    "options": {
      "A": "A concrete implementation of a data structure using a specific programming language.",
      "B": "A mathematical model that defines a data type by its behavior from the point of view of a user, without specifying implementation details.",
      "C": "A collection of variables stored contiguously in memory.",
      "D": "A visual diagram that shows the hierarchy of classes in object‑oriented programming."
    },
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "explanation": "An ADT specifies the logical behavior (operations and their semantics) of a data type, independent of any particular implementation, which matches option B.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the primary difference between a singly linked list and a doubly linked list?",
    "options": {
      "A": "A singly linked list stores data in a contiguous block of memory, while a doubly linked list does not.",
      "B": "A singly linked list allows traversal in only one direction, whereas a doubly linked list permits traversal in both forward and backward directions.",
      "C": "A singly linked list can only hold integer values, while a doubly linked list can hold any data type.",
      "D": "A singly linked list automatically balances itself after each insertion, unlike a doubly linked list."
    },
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "explanation": "In a singly linked list each node has a single pointer to the next node, enabling one‑way traversal. A doubly linked list adds a previous pointer, allowing two‑way traversal, which is described in option B.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "If an array contains n elements, what is the worst‑case time complexity of accessing the element at index k (where 0 ≤ k < n)?",
    "options": {
      "A": "O(log n)",
      "B": "O(n)",
      "C": "O(1)",
      "D": "O(n log n)"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Arrays provide direct (random) access to any index using arithmetic on the base address, so the operation takes constant time, O(1).",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which data structure is most appropriate for implementing a function call stack that follows Last‑In‑First‑Out (LIFO) semantics?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Queue",
      "B": "Binary Search Tree",
      "C": "Stack",
      "D": "Hash Table"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "A stack intrinsically follows LIFO order, making it the natural choice for a call stack; the other structures do not guarantee LIFO behavior.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Consider a binary tree where each node has at most two children. Which of the following statements is FALSE?",
    "options": {
      "A": "The height of a tree with a single node is zero.",
      "B": "A full binary tree with n internal nodes has exactly n + 1 leaf nodes.",
      "C": "In a binary search tree, an inorder traversal yields the keys in sorted order.",
      "D": "A binary tree with n nodes always has exactly n − 1 edges."
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "While a tree with n nodes has n − 1 edges, this holds for any tree, not specifically for binary trees. However, the statement becomes false when considering a binary tree that may be empty (n = 0), where the formula would give –1 edges, which is impossible. Thus option D is the incorrect statement.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  }
]
